---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocCards, Callout, DocsTable } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const CARDS = [
  { icon: '🏛️', title: 'Architecture-First', desc: 'Why OpenFlows plans before it codes, how the planning gate works, and the "when in doubt, don\u2019t approve" rule.', href: '/docs/concepts/architecture-first' },
  { icon: '👥', title: 'The Agent Team', desc: 'NEXUS, FORGE, SENTINEL, VESSEL, and LORE \u2014 their roles, how they work in sequence and in parallel, and how they\u2019re configured.', href: '/docs/concepts/agent-team' },
  { icon: '🔁', title: 'The Ticket Lifecycle', desc: 'How an issue becomes a merged pull request, the fixed phases, and what happens when work gets stuck.', href: '/docs/getting-started/lifecycle-steps' },
  { icon: '🏗️', title: 'The Planning Gate', desc: 'Plan before code: why it\u2019s structural, how the gate is enforced, and why the system blocks rather than guesses.', href: '/docs/concepts/planning-gate' },
  { icon: '🗂️', title: 'The Coordination Store', desc: 'The single record of truth that keeps every agent, ticket, approval, and review consistent.', href: '/docs/concepts/coordination-store' },
  { icon: '🖥️', title: 'Workspaces', desc: 'Where work actually runs: thin, isolated, disposable workspaces with real identity and tight networks.', href: '/docs/concepts/workspaces' },
  { icon: '🏢', title: 'Tenants', desc: 'How one server serves many teams \u2014 what a tenant is, how isolation is enforced, and how you manage one.', href: '/docs/concepts/tenants' },
  { icon: '🧑‍💻', title: 'Human Escalation', desc: 'When a human gets pulled in, what happens on escalation, and the three ways you can resolve it.', href: '/docs/concepts/human-escalation' },
  { icon: '⚙️', title: 'The Agent Registry', desc: 'How the team is configured without code \u2014 roles, models, skills, and tools \u2014 and the zero-capacity safety rule.', href: '/docs/concepts/agent-registry' },
  { icon: '🔗', title: 'The Coder / OpenFlows Split', desc: 'The founding idea: Coder governs where agents run; OpenFlows governs how they coordinate.', href: '/docs/concepts/coder-openflows-split' },
];

const CONCEPT_ROWS = [
  ['The Team', 'Five specialised agents with narrow roles and enforced hand-offs, so no single agent both writes and reviews its own work.', '/docs/concepts/agent-team'],
  ['The Ticket Lifecycle', 'A fixed path from GitHub issue to merged pull request, with recovery and human escalation as part of the loop.', '/docs/getting-started/lifecycle-steps'],
  ['The Planning Gate', 'No code before an approved plan; the gate is structural and single-use approvals can\u2019t be replayed.', '/docs/concepts/planning-gate'],
  ['The Coordination Store', 'A shared, durable record of truth that only the orchestrator and the worker command surface may write.', '/docs/concepts/coordination-store'],
  ['Workspaces', 'Thin, disposable, isolated execution seats with no secrets and real, attributable identity.', '/docs/concepts/workspaces'],
  ['Tenants', 'Strictly separated teams on one server, isolated by both identity and namespaced coordination state.', '/docs/concepts/tenants'],
  ['Human Escalation', 'The system stops and hands you the decisions that need a judgment call, rather than guessing.', '/docs/concepts/human-escalation'],
  ['The Agent Registry', 'Team configuration without code or restarts, with a guardrail against accidentally zeroing a role.', '/docs/concepts/agent-registry'],
  ['The Coder / OpenFlows Split', 'Coder governs where agents run; OpenFlows governs how they coordinate. The founding asymmetry.', '/docs/concepts/coder-openflows-split'],
];
---


<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Core Concepts' }]}>
  <h1>Core Concepts</h1>
  <p>
    OpenFlows runs a disciplined engineering team of AI agents that coordinate through a shared, governed
    system. The concepts in this section are the vocabulary the rest of the documentation uses: who the
    team is, how a ticket travels from issue to merged pull request, why the team plans before it codes,
    where the coordination state lives, where work actually runs, and how security holds together.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are deciding whether OpenFlows fits your organisation, read these pages first. If you are already
    running it, they explain the reasoning behind the behaviour you see in production.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="One idea anchors everything">
    <strong>Coder governs <em>where</em> agents run. OpenFlows governs <em>how</em> they coordinate.</strong>
    This founding split explains the architecture, and the other concepts build on it.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Concepts at a Glance</h2>
  <DocsTable
    headers={['Concept', 'What problem it solves', 'Read more']}
    rows={CONCEPT_ROWS.map(([concept, desc, href]) => [
      `<strong>${concept}</strong>`,
      desc,
      `<a href="${href}">Details \u2192</a>`,
    ])}
  />

  <h2>How the Concepts Fit Together</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is an orchestration layer that turns a self-hosted runtime into an autonomous engineering
    team. The <strong>Coder / OpenFlows split</strong> sets the stage: the runtime provides governed,
    ephemeral <strong>workspaces</strong> and real identity, while OpenFlows supplies the coordination that
    makes them a team. The <strong>agent team</strong> plans, builds, reviews, merges, and documents, and
    each <strong>ticket</strong> moves through a fixed <strong>lifecycle</strong>. The
    <strong>planning gate</strong> keeps every ticket honest by refusing to advance work without an approved
    plan. All of this is held together by the <strong>coordination store</strong>  -  the single source of
    truth  -  and configured through the <strong>agent registry</strong>. <strong>Tenants</strong> isolate
    many teams on one server, and <strong>human escalation</strong> keeps a person in the loop exactly where
    a judgment call is needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to Start</h2>
  <DocCards cards={CARDS} />

  <Callout type="tip" title="Read the split first, then the gate">
    If you're new, start with the Coder / OpenFlows split to get the mental model, then the planning gate to
    see how discipline is enforced. The rest of the concepts fill in the details.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
